New s7_discussion.tex:
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{The linear limit is not the attainable depth}
With a clipped actuator the linear stability limit $\alim(g)$ is an upper bound that a real cut reaches only if it enters gently and is not disturbed. Two consequences follow for practice. First, the way the tool enters the cut is part of the design: a step entry from rest, an idealised full engagement at $t=0$, saturates the actuator for a few tooth periods and, at 157 of the 251 speeds of the case study, pushes cuts below $0.97\,\alim$ into chatter, while a face entry or a feed ramp reaches the limit (Section~\ref{sec:bistability}). Second, the authority that the feedback spends on the forced vibration must be budgeted explicitly. At \SI{2500}{\rpm} and $g=\SI{1500}{\volt\second\per\metre}$ the forced orbit already requires a command of 6--\SI{8.6}{\volt} at about half of the depths below the limit, leaving between a fraction of a volt and \SI{3}{\volt} for the deviation from the orbit, which is what sets the basin. This margin is fragile: for DVF designs that keep \SI{10}{\percent} of the voltage free, 1.1--\SI{4.5}{\micro\metre} of eccentric runout brings the forced command to the clip (Section~\ref{sec:delayed-robust}), so a voltage guard for runout and noise belongs in the authority condition of Section~\ref{sec:feasibility}. The certificates of Section~\ref{sec:certificates} then bound from below, on collocation models, the instantaneous perturbation that the remaining authority can absorb; persistent disturbances are not covered.
% src: README 13.1 (orbit command at 2500 rpm, g = 1500), 8.4, 11.4; results/c4_summary.json (robust.by_rpm, DVF runout_tolerance_um 1.12-4.50)

\paragraph{How conservative the certificates are, and why}
Where the simulated basin is finite, the certified kicks are 1.5 to 28 times smaller than the simulated critical kicks, and about 2.3 times smaller near the limit; where no kick up to \SI{3}{\metre\per\second} caused chatter, they are smaller by a factor of at least 240. From $0.9\alim$ upwards they correspond to 1.4--\SI{8.5}{\micro\metre} of equivalent displacement, the order of the runout that uses up the free voltage, and there the describing-function estimate of Section~\ref{sec:bistab-df} is within 0.88--1.13 of the simulated basin at almost no cost: the certificate gives a guarantee for the model, the describing function a design estimate. The gap lies mainly in the certificate. The critical kick of the collocation model itself is 0.62--0.99 times the simulated one (median 0.92), and for this helical cutter averaging the time-periodic cutting coefficient changes it by only $-3$ to $+5\,\%$ phase by phase; the Floquet limit of the periodic model lies between 0.99999 and 1.0011 times the averaged limit at half immersion, and the periodic certificate is a median of 6\,\% larger than the averaged one. The rest of the gap is attributed to the ellipsoidal shape of the certified set, to the sector bound that replaces the dead zone and, at shallow depths, to the validity bound on the chip, an attribution that was not tested by re-solving. Lur'e-type, piecewise-quadratic or sum-of-squares Lyapunov functions, and Lyapunov--Krasovskii functionals that act on the delay equation itself rather than on its collocation model, are the natural next steps. The step entry from rest is certified only at shallow depths; certifying it at productive depths requires a phase-dependent entry condition that is not available yet.
% src: README 12.4-12.7, 14.5-14.10, results/c3_summary.json errata; results/c2_validate.npz recomputed (19 points without chatter up to 3 m/s: certificates 1.67-12.51 mm/s; certificates at 0.9-0.98 a_lim 1.14-6.89 mm/s, i.e. 1.4-8.5 um at omega_n)

\paragraph{What the delayed term buys and what it costs}
Because $\dot x(t)-\dot x(t-T)$ vanishes on the forced orbit, the delayed term adds damping of the chatter deviation without adding to the forced command. Under the same authority rule as DVF it raises the linear limit by a median factor of 1.78 for $g_2=\SI{2000}{\volt\second\per\metre}$ (1.31 with the usable depth capped at twice the cutter diameter), and the simulated face entry follows the new limit. At the same forced command it widens the simulated basin by a median factor of 1.14 over 22 depth--speed pairs, and 1.8 to 2.6 times where the DVF certificate almost vanishes, so the gain comes from the larger linear margin and not from freed authority; at shallow depths the basin does not widen. The step-entry limit rises by a median factor of only 1.03, and by at most \SI{1.1}{\milli\metre} at \SI{2100}{\rpm} and above, where the entry saturation, not the damping, sets the limit, so the bistable band widens. The price is robustness. The phase of $1-e^{-sT}$ alternates between lead and lag from one tooth-passing harmonic to the next; where it lags, it adds to the loop delay and erodes the phase margin of higher modes. With the assumed second modes, $g_2=\SI{1000}{\volt\second\per\metre}$ tolerates about \SI{200}{\micro\second} of loop delay and $g_2$ of 2000 to \SI{4000}{\volt\second\per\metre} only about \SI{100}{\micro\second}, against \SI{300}{\micro\second} for DVF, on a coarse grid of delays. The delay must also follow the spindle speed: for $g_2=\SI{2000}{\volt\second\per\metre}$ a $+2\,\%$ mismatch lowers the limit to 0.87--0.93 of its nominal value. And the term raises the sensitivity of the forced command to eccentric runout, which the voltage margin then tolerates only up to 1.0--\SI{2.4}{\micro\metre}. A gain $g_2\approx\SI{1000}{\volt\second\per\metre}$ is a reasonable compromise: it keeps 1.2 to 1.6 times the DVF limit and the robustness of a \SI{200}{\micro\second} loop. An implementation would need a fast loop, a multi-mode frequency response in the design, a delay synchronised with the spindle encoder and an authority margin; a low-pass filter in the delay path, as in repetitive control, or DVF with notch filters at the tooth-passing harmonics might keep much of the benefit with less phase lag, but neither was evaluated.
% src: README 13.5-13.10, results/c4_summary.json; results/c4_limits.npz recomputed (median ratio with the depth capped at 32 mm: 1.31); results/c4_validate2.npz via c4_summary.json (same_g1_over_dvf median 1.14, 19/22)

\paragraph{Limitations}
The results are predictions of a model that classifies 17 of the 19 published cutting tests of \citet{ozsoy2026robust} as measured; the two exceptions are controlled cuts that chattered at 0.76 and 0.80 of the predicted limit (Section~\ref{sec:model-case}). The model has one identified mode, uniform over the depth, a rigid tool, a cutting force linear in the chip thickness without edge forces or process damping, and an actuator whose only nonlinearity is a hard clip on an exact velocity, without stroke or current limits, amplifier dynamics, sensor noise or, outside Section~\ref{sec:delayed-robust}, loop delay; the second modes of Section~\ref{sec:delayed-robust} are assumed. The attribution of the bistability to the clip holds for this linear force law, since a nonlinear force law can itself make the cut subcritical \citep{kalmarnagy2001subcritical,dombovari2008estimates}. Outside the basin of the forced orbit the model predicts clipped chatter of 0.4--\SI{0.6}{\milli\metre}, or divergence, beyond its range of validity. The free voltage depends on what the model omits: runout uses it up (Section~\ref{sec:delayed-robust}), and edge forces would add to the static force and hence to the forced command. The certificates hold for collocation models and for instantaneous perturbations only. The 300-revolution classifier biases boundaries near $\alim$ high (Section~\ref{sec:bistab-map} and \ref{app:simulator}).
% src: results/cuts_main.json; README 7, 11.2, 11.7, 12.7, 13.10; verification/tdsim/check8, check9, check10

\paragraph{Towards experimental validation}
The predictions could be tested in two stages. On a hardware-in-the-loop rig, where the real actuator, amplifier and controller act on the identified structure while the regenerative force is emulated \citep{sahu2021bistability,sahu2023adaptive,mancisidor2019mechatronics}, command pulses applied through the actuator at controlled phases, with and without the clip, would measure the critical pulse against the depth directly and repeatably. Cutting tests at two or three speeds where the predicted band is wide would then compare a face entry with an entry into a pre-machined pocket, or a feed hold and restart inside the cut, at depths inside the band, with the runout and the settled command measured, chatter detected from once-per-tooth samples, and enough repetitions to estimate probabilities, since uncontrolled disturbances make the outcome random.

